Searching by Meaning (Semantic Search)

Day 9: Learned how close *two* sentences are.
Day 10: I search through *lots* of sentences to find the closest ones to my question.
This is the "search" part that RAG needs

The simple idea
Normal search matches **words**.
If I search "log in" it only finds text that literally contains "log in".

Semantic search matches **meaning**.
So "can't log in" can find "authentication failed" — even though they don't share a single word.

Think of it like this:
Old search = matching spelling.
Semantic search = matching *what you actually meant*.

How it works (in steps)
1. Take all my documents and turn each one into a vector (a list of numbers = its meaning). I do this **once**.
2. When someone asks a question, turn the **question** into a vector too.
3. Compare the question's vector to every document's vector (using cosine similarity from Day 9).
4. Show the few documents with the **highest** scores.

The code (with comments)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using OllamaSharp;

// The thing that turns text into number-lists (meaning)
IEmbeddingGenerator<string, Embedding<float>> embedder =
    new OllamaApiClient(new Uri("http://localhost:11434"), "nomic-embed-text");

// A saved document = its text + its meaning (vector)
record Doc(string Text, float[] Vector);

// STEP 1: my list of documents
string[] corpus =
{
    "User authentication failed - invalid token",
    "Database connection timeout after 30s",
    "Payment gateway returned 502",
    "Null reference in OrderService.Calculate",
    "Disk space exceeded on node-3",
};

// Turn each document into a vector ONCE and remember it
var index = new List<Doc>();
foreach (var text in corpus)
    index.Add(new Doc(text, (await embedder.GenerateVectorAsync(text)).ToArray()));

// STEP 2-4: search by meaning
async Task<List<(string Text, float Score)>> Search(string query, int topK)
{
    // turn the question into a vector
    float[] q = (await embedder.GenerateVectorAsync(query)).ToArray();

    return index
        .Select(d => (d.Text, Score: Cosine(q, d.Vector)))  // score each document
        .OrderByDescending(x => x.Score)                     // best matches first
        .Take(topK)                                          // keep the top few
        .ToList();
}

// Show the 3 closest matches to "can't log in"
foreach (var hit in await Search("can't log in", 3))
    Console.WriteLine($"{hit.Score:F3}  {hit.Text}");

// Closeness score (from Day 9). Closer to 1 = more similar.
static float Cosine(float[] x, float[] y)
{
    float dot = 0, mx = 0, my = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < x.Length; i++) { dot += x[i]*y[i]; mx += x[i]*x[i]; my += y[i]*y[i]; }
    return dot / (MathF.Sqrt(mx) * MathF.Sqrt(my));
}
```

**What happens when I run it:**
I asked for `"can't log in"` and the top result was **"User authentication failed - invalid token"** —
even though they share **no words at all**. Old keyword search would have found nothing.

Search checks the question against **every single document, one by one.**
If a few thousand documents? No problem.
If a few **million**? Now it's slow and expensive, because it checks all of them every time.
That's exactly why **vector databases** exist. They use clever shortcuts to find the closest matches
super fast without checking every single one.
Simple way to remember: a vector database is just a **faster, smarter version of my `List<Doc>`.**
(And spotting "this gets slow at scale" is easy for me — that's my performance background talking.)

Tools I'd use later (instead of writing it by hand)
| Tool | Use it when |
|---|---|
| **InMemoryVectorStore** | Just practicing / small data |
| **Qdrant** | Local testing (runs in Docker) |
| **Azure AI Search** | Real production app |
| **pgvector** | I already use PostgreSQL |
| **SQL Server 2025** | I already use SQL Server |

They all do the same job by storing vectors and find the closest ones — just faster and saved to disk.

Why this matters for my project
Imagine saving **every past error and how it was fixed.**
When a new error happens, I search by meaning and instantly see:
*"We've seen something like this before — here's what fixed it."*
Even if the new error is worded completely differently. That's a real, useful tool.

Key takeaways:
1. **Semantic search finds by meaning, not exact words.**
2. **The steps are simple:** turn everything into vectors → turn the question into a vector → pick the closest ones.
3. **It really works:** "can't log in" found "authentication failed" with no shared words.
4. **Checking every document gets slow** when there are millions of them.
5. **Vector databases fix the speed problem** — think of one as a fast, saved version of my list.
6. **I can swap tools later** without rewriting my app.
7. **This is the "search" step of RAG**

one line
Semantic search = turn everything into meaning-numbers, then find the ones closest to my question.
